    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Gravel is one of the most cost-effective, hard-wearing and versatile driveway surfaces you can choose. Whether you are building a new home on a rural plot or replacing a crumbling tarmac drive in town, gravel gives you a permeable surface that copes with cars and changing weather well. It is usually cheaper to install than asphalt or concrete, though it needs more regular top-ups over the years.</p>
<p>What it actually costs varies a great deal with your region, the supplier, the year and the site, so rather than quote figures that will quickly date, this guide explains what makes one gravel driveway cost more than another. That way you can plan sensibly, read quotes with confidence and end up with a drive that does not need constant, expensive repairs.</p>

<h2 id="size">1. Size and depth</h2>
<p>The most obvious factor is the area. Gravel is sold by weight or volume, so a longer, wider drive needs more of it. There are economies of scale, though. Contractors and suppliers have fixed costs, such as bringing machinery to site and delivery charges, that are much the same whether the job is small or large.</p>
<p>That means a short drive can cost more per square metre than a long one, because those fixed costs are spread over a smaller area. On a long country drive, the cost per square metre often falls once the job reaches a certain size.</p>
<p>Depth matters as much as area. A drive is built in layers, and most of the material sits in the compacted sub-base you never see. For cars, that is commonly around 150–200 mm (6–8 in), with more on soft ground; your installer sets it for your soil. A deeper build uses noticeably more stone and more digging, but skimping on depth is the surest way to pay twice.</p>

<h2 id="materials">2. Materials</h2>
<p>Not all gravel is the same, and the stone you choose affects both the look and how long the drive lasts. A typical gravel driveway has three layers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Bottom layer:</strong> on soft or wet ground, larger stone around 40–75 mm gives a strong, free-draining foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Sub-base:</strong> crushed stone with fines, sold in the UK as Type 1 sub-base or MOT Type 1 and in the US as crusher run or dense-grade aggregate. It compacts into a firm, solid layer and is one of the most affordable materials on the job.</li>
<li><strong>Top layer:</strong> the stone you see. Angular crushed stone of around 20 mm (¾ in) in a local stone such as limestone or granite is usually the most economical and practical choice. Rounded pea gravel looks soft and pretty but moves under tyres. Coloured gravels, such as golden or buff-toned stone, and white marble chippings cost noticeably more and are usually chosen for looks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Distance from the quarry also matters. A stone quarried locally is generally cheaper than one transported a long way, so if you love a particular decorative stone, ask your supplier about a local alternative in a similar colour.</p>

<h2 id="preparation">3. Site preparation</h2>
<p>You cannot simply tip gravel onto grass and expect it to stay put. Groundwork is often the hidden cost of a gravel drive. It covers clearing the site of shrubs, roots and old surfaces, and, most importantly, digging out the turf and topsoil, which are too soft to carry a car.</p>
<p>If the ground is uneven, moving soil from high spots to low ones adds time, and taking spoil away adds skip or haulage costs. An old concrete or tarmac drive that has to be broken out first adds noticeably to the bill. On larger sites, contractors use mini diggers and other machinery to strip the ground back to firm subsoil, which is quicker but needs a skilled operator. Before any digging, buried services need to be located: in the UK, check utility plans or use a cable avoidance tool; in the US, call 811.</p>

<h2 id="drainage">4. Drainage</h2>
<p>Drainage has more effect on how long a gravel driveway lasts than almost anything else. If water is allowed to pool, it washes out the fines and creates potholes.</p>
<p>Every drive needs a crown or cross-fall, a gentle fall to the edges that your installer sets, so water runs off to the sides. On wet sites, heavy clay or slopes, you may need more:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Filter drains (French drains):</strong> perforated pipes in stone-filled trenches that carry water away.</li>
<li><strong>Culverts:</strong> pipes under the entrance that let an existing ditch keep flowing. If the ditch runs along a public road, check with your local highway authority, as works there may need their consent.</li>
<li><strong>Swales and soakaways:</strong> shallow grassed channels or underground soakaways that hold runoff and let it soak into the ground.</li>
</ul>
<p>Drainage works can add noticeably to the upfront cost, but they are usually far cheaper than repairing a washed-out drive later. Gravel itself is permeable, which helps: in England, a new or replacement hard surface over 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area, and gravel usually qualifies. A new dropped kerb needs council permission. Check with your local authority before you start.</p>

<h2 id="underlayment">5. Membranes and grids</h2>
<p>A geotextile membrane, laid on the prepared ground under the stone, is easy to leave out to save money. It stops the stone sinking into the soil and the soil working up into the stone, and it slows weeds coming up from below. Plastic gravel grids, laid near the surface, hold the top stone in place and help on slopes and turning areas.</p>
<p>Both add modestly to materials and labour, more so for grids, but on clay or soft ground they reduce how much top-up stone you need over the years. Ask your installer whether your ground needs them.</p>

<h2 id="labour">6. Labour</h2>
<p>Unless you have access to a tipper lorry, a mini digger, a compactor and plenty of stamina, you will probably hire a professional. Labour is a large part of the cost, and it may be priced as a day rate or as a fixed price for the whole job. How much depends on:</p>
<ul>
<li>The amount of digging and spoil removal.</li>
<li>Access: a narrow side entrance or no room for a lorry to tip means more barrowing and more time.</li>
<li>Drainage works and edging, which add skilled time.</li>
<li>The number of layers and how carefully each one is compacted.</li>
</ul>
<p>A professional installation includes setting the levels for drainage, spreading each layer evenly and compacting it with a plate compactor or roller. Poorly compacted stone moves under tyres, ruts quickly and stays unstable for months, so this is not the place to save.</p>

<h2 id="edging">7. Edging</h2>
<p>Edging is easy to forget in a budget, but it stops stone spreading into borders, onto the lawn and out onto the pavement. Treated timber or plastic edging is usually the cheapest. Steel edging costs a little more. Setts, granite blocks or brick bedded in concrete cost noticeably more but look smart and last well, and a paved apron at the entrance helps keep stone off the road.</p>

<h2 id="maintenance">8. Ongoing maintenance</h2>
<p>Gravel is cheap to install, but it is a moving surface, so factor in the running costs. Tyres push stone to the edges, rain shifts the fines and some stone is carried away on wheels and boots.</p>
<p>Expect to buy a fresh layer of top stone every few years, depending on traffic and weather, and to regrade the surface from time to time to smooth out ripples and fill potholes. On a short drive that is easy to do yourself with a rake; on a long one you may hire a contractor with a grading blade. Our <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-maintenance-by-season">seasonal gravel driveway maintenance guide</a> sets out what to do and when.</p>

<h2 id="comparing-quotes">How to get driveway quotes you can compare</h2>
<p>Quotes are only comparable if they price the same job. Give two or three installers the same measurements and specification, and ask for an itemised quote covering:</p>
<ul>
<li>The area, and the depth of each layer.</li>
<li>The stone for each layer: type, size, colour and quantity.</li>
<li>Excavation, removal of an old surface, and skip or spoil removal.</li>
<li>Geotextile membrane or gravel grids.</li>
<li>Drainage: the fall, plus any filter drains, culverts or soakaways.</li>
<li>Edging, and any paved apron at the entrance.</li>
<li>Compaction equipment and the number of passes.</li>
<li>Planning, dropped kerb or highway applications, and who makes them.</li>
<li>Making good of lawns and borders afterwards.</li>
</ul>
<p>If one quote is much lower than the rest, check which of these items it leaves out, especially depth, membrane and drainage.</p>

<h2 id="keeping-costs-down">Ways to keep the cost down</h2>
<ul>
<li>Choose a local crushed stone for the top layer rather than a decorative stone from far away.</li>
<li>Keep the drive a simple shape; curves and extra parking bays add area and edging.</li>
<li>Combine the drive with other groundwork in the garden so you pay for machinery and delivery once.</li>
<li>Reuse sound existing edging or kerbs where your installer confirms they are in good condition.</li>
<li>Do the light jobs yourself, such as clearing the site of plants or spreading the final top layer, and leave the excavation, base and compaction to a professional.</li>
<li>Spend where it matters: a properly compacted base and good drainage cost less than repeated repairs.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Ask installers to price the top stone as a separate line. You can then compare a standard crushed stone and a decorative gravel on the same quote and see exactly what the upgrade adds.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Is a gravel driveway cheaper than asphalt?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes. Gravel is usually cheaper to install than asphalt or concrete, though it needs more regular top-ups. A sealed surface needs less day-to-day care, but gravel is easier and cheaper to patch when it ruts or sinks.</p>
<h3>How long does a gravel driveway last?</h3>
<p>That depends on how well it was built, how much traffic it takes, your climate and how well it is maintained. A gravel drive with a good base and good drainage can last for many years, and because it is flexible, it can be topped up and regraded rather than replaced when ground movement or frost would crack a solid surface.</p>
<h3>Can I build a gravel driveway myself?</h3>
<p>A small parking area is a realistic DIY project. For a full driveway, hiring machinery, arranging deliveries and doing the compaction can add up to a large part of a professional's cost, and an experienced installer will usually finish faster and get the drainage right. If you hire heavy machinery, follow the hire company's safety guidance or hire it with an operator.</p>
<h3>What is the best type of gravel for a driveway?</h3>
<p>For the base, crushed stone with fines (Type 1 sub-base in the UK, crusher run or dense-grade aggregate in the US) compacts into a solid layer. For the surface, angular crushed stone of around 20 mm (¾ in) is stable and practical. Pea gravel suits decorative paths and borders better than a drive.</p>
<h3>How do I stop weeds growing in my gravel?</h3>
<p>A heavy-duty geotextile membrane under the stone stops most weeds coming up from the soil. For seeds that land on top, regular raking and hand weeding work well. If you use a weedkiller, follow the label and keep it away from drains, ponds and watercourses; if you use a flame weeder, follow the instructions and never use it in dry conditions or near plants, fences or fuel.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>The cost of a gravel driveway comes down to its size and depth, the stone, the groundwork and drainage, and how much labour the site takes, plus a little upkeep every few years. An itemised quote based on the same specification is the clearest way to see where your money goes. Before you commit, read the common <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-mistakes">gravel driveway mistakes</a> to avoid, and if you are still choosing a surface, compare the <a href="/blog/paver-driveway-pros-and-cons">pros and cons of a paver driveway</a>. You can also explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your front garden with a designer.</p>
